---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import '../styles/marketing.css';

// Distinct from the homepage title on purpose: the homepage owns the product
// query, this page targets the informational "why self-host" query.
const title = 'Why Self-Host AI Coding Agents? A Practical Guide | GratefulAgents';
const description =
  'Why teams move coding agents off laptops and vendor clouds onto their own infrastructure, what it takes to run them, and how GratefulAgents does it.';

const structuredData = [
  {
    '@type': 'TechArticle',
    '@id': 'https://gratefulagents.dev/self-hosted-coding-agents/#article',
    headline: title,
    description,
    url: 'https://gratefulagents.dev/self-hosted-coding-agents/',
    author: {'@type': 'Organization', '@id': 'https://gratefulagents.dev/#organization'},
    publisher: {'@id': 'https://gratefulagents.dev/#organization'},
  },
  {
    '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
    '@id': 'https://gratefulagents.dev/self-hosted-coding-agents/#breadcrumb',
    itemListElement: [
      {'@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: 'https://gratefulagents.dev/'},
      {
        '@type': 'ListItem',
        position: 2,
        name: 'Why self-host',
        item: 'https://gratefulagents.dev/self-hosted-coding-agents/',
      },
    ],
  },
  {
    '@type': 'HowTo',
    '@id': 'https://gratefulagents.dev/self-hosted-coding-agents/#howto',
    name: 'Run your first self-hosted coding agent on a server',
    description:
      'Install GratefulAgents on a fresh Debian or Ubuntu server with k3s and run a first agent against a GitHub repository.',
    step: [
      {
        '@type': 'HowToStep',
        position: 1,
        name: 'Get a server',
        text: 'Start a fresh Debian or Ubuntu machine, such as a cloud VM, with sudo access.',
        url: 'https://gratefulagents.dev/docs/getting-started/self-hosting-k3s/',
      },
      {
        '@type': 'HowToStep',
        position: 2,
        name: 'Run the installer',
        text: 'Clone github.com/gratefulagents/gratefulagents and run `make k3s-install`. It installs k3s, the chart, PostgreSQL, MinIO, and Jaeger, then prints the dashboard URL and admin login.',
        url: 'https://gratefulagents.dev/docs/getting-started/self-hosting-k3s/',
      },
      {
        '@type': 'HowToStep',
        position: 3,
        name: 'Add a model credential',
        text: 'In the dashboard, open Settings, then Credentials, and add a key for Claude, OpenAI, or another supported provider.',
        url: 'https://gratefulagents.dev/docs/settings/credentials/',
      },
      {
        '@type': 'HowToStep',
        position: 4,
        name: 'Connect a repository',
        text: 'Create a project for a GitHub repository and add a GitHub entry point so issues and comments can start runs.',
        url: 'https://gratefulagents.dev/docs/integrations/github/',
      },
      {
        '@type': 'HowToStep',
        position: 5,
        name: 'Start a run and read the trace',
        text: 'Label an issue or start a run from the dashboard, follow it in Agent Ops, and open its trace when it finishes.',
        url: 'https://gratefulagents.dev/docs/runs/start-a-run/',
      },
    ],
  },
];
---

<Base {title} {description} type="article" {structuredData}>
  <main id="main">
    <div class="mk-page">
      <nav aria-label="Breadcrumb">
        <ol class="mk-breadcrumb">
          <li><a href="/">Home</a></li>
          <li><span aria-current="page">Why self-host</span></li>
        </ol>
      </nav>

      <div class="mk-hero">
        <h1>Where should coding agents run?</h1>
        <p>
          Most teams start with an agent in a terminal on someone's laptop. Then they try a hosted
          agent from a vendor. Both work for a while. This page is about the third option, running
          agents on <strong>infrastructure you control</strong>, and what you actually sign up for
          when you do.
        </p>
        <div class="cta-row">
          <a href="/docs/getting-started/self-hosting-k3s/" class="btn btn-primary">Deploy to a server</a>
          <a href="/differences/" class="text-link">Compare with Devin and Copilot</a>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="mk-layout-2col">
        <article class="mk-prose">
          <h2 id="options">Three places an agent can live</h2>
          <p>
            A coding agent reads a repository, runs commands, and writes code back, often for an
            hour or more. Where that loop runs decides how many you can have, who can start them,
            and who can see what they did.
          </p>
          <h3>On a laptop</h3>
          <p>
            Fast to try and fine for one person and one task. It stops when the machine sleeps,
            competes with you for CPU and the working copy, and keeps its history in a terminal.
            Running five at once means five terminals and a lot of <code>git worktree</code>.
          </p>
          <h3>In a vendor's cloud</h3>
          <p>
            Runs keep going without you and the vendor handles capacity. In exchange, the
            checkout, the agent process, and the model credentials sit on their machines, you use
            the models they offer, and you see the parts of each run they choose to show.
          </p>
          <h3>On your own infrastructure</h3>
          <p>
            Runs keep going without you, as with a vendor, but in your cloud account and next to
            your private services. You choose the models and hold the keys, and every run leaves a
            full record. The price is that you operate it.
          </p>

          <h2 id="why">Why teams pick the third</h2>

          <h3>They want more than one agent</h3>
          <p>
            Once agents are useful, people want them on every small issue, on a schedule, and on
            review duty. That is a scheduling problem: queueing, isolation between runs, resource
            limits, timeouts, cleanup. A cluster already solves it for services, and agent runs can
            use the same machinery.
          </p>

          <h3>Code and credentials have rules</h3>
          <p>
            Regulation, customer contracts, or plain internal policy often limit where source code
            may go. Self-hosting takes the agent vendor out of that path. The checkout, the sandbox,
            the tool calls, and the run history stay in your account.
          </p>
          <div class="mk-callout mk-callout-warn">
            <p>
              <strong>Be precise about inference.</strong> Unless you point GratefulAgents at a model
              endpoint you host, prompts and the code inside them still go to the provider you
              configure (Claude, OpenAI, OpenRouter, Grok, or GitHub Copilot) under that provider's
              terms. What changes is that you choose the provider, you hold the contract and the
              key, and each run's trace shows exactly what was sent. If nothing may leave at all,
              you need self-hosted inference as well.
            </p>
          </div>

          <h3>Agents need to reach private things</h3>
          <p>
            Real work touches private package registries, internal APIs, and staging databases.
            A hosted agent needs tunnels or firewall exceptions for each one. An agent in your own
            network reaches them the way your services already do, under the network policies you
            already have.
          </p>

          <h3>Model spend under your own contract</h3>
          <p>
            Hosted agents fold inference into their price. With your own keys, your existing
            Anthropic, OpenAI, or OpenRouter terms apply, and per-run cost and token counts show
            which tasks and which models the money goes to.
          </p>

          <h3>A record of what the agent did</h3>
          <p>
            When an agent changes code, someone will eventually ask what it read, what it ran, and
            why. A summary feed doesn't answer that. A trace of every model call and tool call
            does, and you can compare the same task across models.
          </p>

          <h3>The setup lives in Git</h3>
          <p>
            On Kubernetes, the agent platform is a Helm chart plus custom resources. Triggers,
            limits, skills, and policies can be reviewed, diffed, and rolled back like the rest of
            your infrastructure.
          </p>

          <h2 id="requirements">What you have to build, or adopt</h2>
          <p>
            Running agents yourself means solving six problems. They are worth knowing whether you
            use GratefulAgents or build your own. Each one below says what the problem is and how
            GratefulAgents handles it.
          </p>

          <div class="mk-feature-grid">
            <div class="mk-feature-card">
              <h3>Isolated sandboxes</h3>
              <p>
                Each run needs a place to execute code that is cut off from other runs and from the
                rest of the cluster, with a fresh filesystem and controlled network access.
              </p>
              <p class="mk-answer">
                <span class="mk-answer-label">GratefulAgents</span>
                One pod per run, provided by
                <a href="https://github.com/kubernetes-sigs/agent-sandbox" rel="noopener noreferrer">kubernetes-sigs/agent-sandbox</a>.
                A RuntimeProfile sets permissions, egress, and an optional runtime class such as gVisor.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="mk-feature-card">
              <h3>Capacity and limits</h3>
              <p>
                Ten agents at once must not starve each other or the cluster, and a stuck run must
                not hold resources forever.
              </p>
              <p class="mk-answer">
                <span class="mk-answer-label">GratefulAgents</span>
                CPU and memory requests and limits per run, a default timeout, caps on concurrent
                runs per cluster and per namespace, and a timeout for stale runs. Optional warm pools
                cut start-up time.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="mk-feature-card">
              <h3>Credentials</h3>
              <p>
                Model keys, GitHub tokens, and registry credentials have to reach the sandbox
                without ending up in logs, traces, or other people's runs.
              </p>
              <p class="mk-answer">
                <span class="mk-answer-label">GratefulAgents</span>
                Kubernetes secrets in your cluster, scoped per project. Model calls go straight to the
                provider and are not proxied through anyone else.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="mk-feature-card">
              <h3>Triggers</h3>
              <p>
                Agents are most useful when real events start them: an issue, a review comment, a
                ticket, a Slack message, a schedule. Each needs a receiver and authentication.
              </p>
              <p class="mk-answer">
                <span class="mk-answer-label">GratefulAgents</span>
                <code>GitHubRepository</code>, <code>LinearProject</code>, <code>SlackAgent</code>,
                and <code>Cron</code> are custom resources, with allowlists for who may start a run.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="mk-feature-card">
              <h3>Observability</h3>
              <p>
                Without a structured record of each run, figuring out why an agent behaved badly is
                guesswork.
              </p>
              <p class="mk-answer">
                <span class="mk-answer-label">GratefulAgents</span>
                Every run sends a trace to the Jaeger instance the chart installs. The
                <a href="/docs/results/observability/">observability views</a> show cost, tokens,
                tool calls, subagents, compactions, and errors, broken down by model.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="mk-feature-card">
              <h3>A way for people to step in</h3>
              <p>
                Agents make mistakes. Someone needs to watch a run, redirect it, or stop it, often
                from somewhere other than a desk.
              </p>
              <p class="mk-answer">
                <span class="mk-answer-label">GratefulAgents</span>
                Interactive, plan, and autopilot modes, switchable mid-run. Steer or stop from the
                web dashboard, the desktop app, iOS, Android, or the Slack thread that started it.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>

          <h2 id="start">Getting a first run going</h2>
          <p>
            The quickest honest test is to install it on a spare server and point it at a
            repository you don't mind an agent touching. Expect about fifteen minutes.
          </p>

          <ol class="mk-steps">
            <li>
              <div>
                <span class="mk-step-title">Get a server</span>
                <span class="mk-step-desc">A fresh Debian or Ubuntu machine with sudo, such as a cloud VM. The <a href="/docs/getting-started/self-hosting-k3s/">k3s guide</a> lists the sizing and network requirements.</span>
              </div>
            </li>
            <li>
              <div>
                <span class="mk-step-title">Run the installer</span>
                <span class="mk-step-desc">Clone the repository and run <code>make k3s-install</code>. It installs k3s, the chart, PostgreSQL with pgvector, MinIO, and Jaeger, then prints the dashboard URL and the admin login.</span>
              </div>
            </li>
            <li>
              <div>
                <span class="mk-step-title">Add a model key</span>
                <span class="mk-step-desc">In <a href="/docs/settings/credentials/">Settings → Credentials</a>, add a key for Claude, OpenAI, or another supported provider.</span>
              </div>
            </li>
            <li>
              <div>
                <span class="mk-step-title">Connect a repository</span>
                <span class="mk-step-desc">Create a project for the repository and add a <a href="/docs/integrations/github/">GitHub entry point</a> so issues and comments can start runs.</span>
              </div>
            </li>
            <li>
              <div>
                <span class="mk-step-title">Start a run and read the trace</span>
                <span class="mk-step-desc">Label an issue or start a run from the dashboard. Follow it in Agent Ops, then open its trace.</span>
              </div>
            </li>
          </ol>
          <p>
            Want to look before renting a machine? The <a href="/docs/getting-started/self-hosting-kind/">Kind guide</a>
            runs the same chart on a macOS or Linux laptop with Docker.
          </p>

          <div class="mk-callout">
            <p>
              <strong>Current status.</strong> GratefulAgents is in early development. Bugs are
              expected and releases may break things. The supported installer targets a single-node
              server; high availability, managed hosting, and a support SLA are not offered. Try it
              on work you can afford to redo.
            </p>
          </div>

          <h2 id="differences">Compared with hosted agents</h2>
          <p>
            Devin and the GitHub Copilot coding agent run on their vendors' infrastructure.
            GratefulAgents runs the same kind of work on yours. The
            <a href="/differences/">comparison page</a> lays it out row by row.
          </p>
        </article>

        <aside>
          <div class="mk-aside-inner">
            <nav class="mk-sidebar-card mk-toc" aria-label="On this page">
              <h3>On this page</h3>
              <ol>
                <li><a href="#options">Three places an agent can live</a></li>
                <li><a href="#why">Why teams pick the third</a></li>
                <li><a href="#requirements">What you have to build</a></li>
                <li><a href="#start">Getting a first run going</a></li>
                <li><a href="#differences">Compared with hosted agents</a></li>
              </ol>
            </nav>
            <div class="mk-sidebar-card">
              <h3>Try it</h3>
              <p>One server, about fifteen minutes, and you have a dashboard and a login.</p>
              <div class="cta-row">
                <a href="/docs/getting-started/self-hosting-k3s/" class="btn btn-primary">Deploy to a server</a>
                <a href="/docs/getting-started/self-hosting-kind/" class="btn btn-quiet">Try on a laptop</a>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </aside>
      </div>
    </div>
  </main>
</Base>
